// D0187 (PROPOSED — NOT YET ACCEPTED) — D0187: adversarial panel review is a declared, portable process
// Recorded via `keel record decision` (D0105 RMWX axis; issue054). Acceptance is a separate explicit
// human gate (method=confirmation, D0106): flip status + add the d0187Accept event on sign-off.
package Decision0187 {
    private import EngineElement::*;
    private import EngineWork::*;
    private import EngineVerification::*;
    private import EngineRelationships::*;

    // #ProspectiveChange (D0070) - governs the addition of .engine/processes/adversarial-panel-review.sysml
    #ProspectiveChange part d0187 : Decision {
        :>> id = "8022c383-6030-445e-b899-65a9fbe333cf";
        :>> title = "D0187: adversarial panel review is a declared, portable process";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-08-21";
        :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> status = DecisionStatus::accepted;
        :>> context = "The production-readiness design (docs/proposals/2026-08-21-production-readiness-design.md) was hardened by a 9-persona adversarial panel run iteratively to uncompelled unanimity: round 1 produced 2 ACCEPT / 7 OBJECT with 60+ findings, round 2 - after a full revision - 5 ACCEPT / 4 OBJECT, round 3 - after a second revision - 9 ACCEPT / 0 OBJECT. The panel caught a factually false premise the author had laundered from a stale tracked requirement (the release channel exists and had shipped four verified releases), a K6 seam three critics found independently (the Bash keel-exemption would have let an agent record the human's sign-off), an unimplementable three-way merge, and a deadlock between a proposed deny tier and the Stop hook. The human then directed: export this critique process to keel, so it can reuse. Today the method lives only in conversation history - exactly what D0040 forbids for recurring work.";
        :>> decision = "PROPOSED: declare the method as .engine/processes/adversarial-panel-review.sysml (five steps: convene, dispatch, revise, converge, record) with a deploying skill at .engine/skills/adversarial-panel-review/SKILL.md registered in the skills registry, and an entry in process-enforcement.toml declaring it unenforceable-by-design (a critique round is a judgment; a guard could only check that findings are non-empty, which would enforce nothing). The panel roster spans failure-seeking (pessimists), value-defense and anti-dilution (optimists), correctness/consistency/mechanism pedantry, scope reduction, and implementation-free kernel derivation (systems engineer). Every panelist prompt carries the honesty rule - persona is a lens, not a script: no manufactured objections, no capitulation - and the output contract: numbered findings each with severity, evidence verified against the repository, and the specific change that resolves it, plus a verdict of ACCEPT or OBJECT naming the minimal flip set. Rounds dispatch all critics in parallel as independent contexts; from round 2 each receives the revised artifact plus a digest of all panelists' prior positions so they rebut each other; the author resolves every flip condition in a revision carrying a finding-to-resolution map; convergence is every panelist independently returning ACCEPT.";
        :>> rationale = "The panel works for a reason the repo has already recorded: a skill is a linter and instructions are ignorable (D0106 violations, issues 082-085), but a critic whose acceptance is CONDITIONAL on named, verifiable changes creates an action-space loop - the author cannot exit until the objections are resolved or refuted with evidence. The honesty rule is the load-bearing part: without it, pessimists manufacture objections forever and optimists capitulate immediately, and unanimity means nothing. The 2026-08-14 panel precedent (.tracking/panel-critiques.sysml) fixes the other honesty constraint this process must carry permanently: same-model subagent panelists are a rigor FRAMING, not independent critics - their critiques record critiquedBy = aiModel and satisfy no critic-independence requirement, and the process's recorded output claims convergence, never independence.";
        :>> consequences = "IF ACCEPTED: the process catalogue grows by one, with a deploying skill (process-skill guard stays green) and a declared unenforceable entry (the hardening view reports it as declared, not as an undeclared gap). Any keel project can run keel process export adversarial-panel-review and reuse the method; the console can launch it once declared. COSTS, stated honestly: a full three-round nine-critic panel is expensive (this one consumed roughly two million tokens of subagent work) - the skill therefore scales the roster to the stakes and names when NOT to use it (small changes, judgment-free mechanical work). REMOVAL PATH per D0163: delete the process file, the skill directory, the registry entry, and the process-enforcement entry; nothing else reads them.";
    }

    // acceptance event (D0121 review-queue sign-off; D0066/D0106 — human-judged, not fabricated)
    verification d0187Accept : Test { :>> id = "bed76d52-e417-42b0-9a9d-372964e86989"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::confirmation; :>> procedureText = "Their words, verbatim: 'accepted all'. Said in response to the presented queue of fifteen proposed Decisions - D0174, D0175, D0176, D0177, D0178, D0179, D0180, D0181, D0182, D0183, D0184, D0185, D0186, D0187, D0188 - so 'all' is that queue and this acceptance covers this Decision as one of the fifteen. The open fork stated in the proposal (section 8-A, strict default for provably empty directories) was named to them alongside the queue with the invitation to object on D0174; they accepted without objection, so D0174 is accepted AS WRITTEN including its stated strict-for-empty default."; }
    part d0187AcceptR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "c5eb8e1a-501b-4980-a8dc-b5d459a3944a"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "10b66bd"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-21"; :>> judgedBy = "wweatherholtz"; }
}
